<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Souvanna Phouma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Souvanna Phouma was a Lao statesman of the royal family. A member of the Lao Issara nationalist government in 1945, he later served several times as prime minister of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> between 1951 and 1975, seeking neutrality and coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>, led by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>. He stayed on as adviser after the communist takeover of 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/souvanna-phouma.jpg" alt="Souvanna Phouma" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marion S. Trikosko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vice_President_Spiro_Agnew_and_Prince_Souvanna_Phouma_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Souvanna Phouma was born on 7 October 1901, the son of Bounkhong, the last vice-king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, and a nephew of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisavang-vong">Sisavang Vong</a>. He received a French education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a>, where he gained a degree in architecture and engineering. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> in 1931, married Aline Claire Allard, daughter of a French father and a Lao mother, and entered the Public Works Service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>.</p><p>Around the end of the war he joined the Lao Issara, or Free Laos, movement with his brother Prince Phetsarath Rattanavongsa and his half-brother Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>. The movement was set up to oppose the French occupation and the provisional government at Vientiane of 1945. When the French reoccupied Laos he fled into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, and he came back in 1949 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> began to concede autonomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1951 Souvanna became prime minister under the banner of the National Progressive Party and held the post until 1954. After the elections of December 1955 he returned to office on a platform of national reconciliation. In August 1956 he and the Communist Pathet Lao, headed by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>, agreed on broad proposals for a government of national union. In the elections of May 1958 for 21 extra assembly seats, parties aligned with the Pathet Lao won 13. Souphanouvong entered the government as Economic Minister, and Phoumi Vongvichit took the Ministry of Religion and Fine Arts.</p><p>In June 1958 the rightists forced Souvanna to resign again. King Sisavang Vatthana signed Royal Ordinance No. 282 dismissing his government and gave power provisionally to a Revolutionary Committee, and Royal Ordinance No. 283 approved a provisional government under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boun-oum">Boun Oum</a>, who acted as front man for Phoui Sananikone. The king asked three princes to form a coalition between the rightists and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>, but it collapsed and the Laotian Civil War began. Souvanna was prime minister again in 1960 and from 1962 to 1975, and between May and August 1960 he was president of the National Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He led the neutralist faction throughout these years, seeking a settlement between the rightists and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>. After the civil war he became an adviser to the new government. He died in Vientiane on 10 January 1984. His honours included the Order of the Million Elephants and the White Parasol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and the French Legion of Honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Souvanna Phouma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Neutralist, rightist and communist factions competed, and the Geneva conference of 1962 created a coalition under <b>Souvanna Phouma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li><li><blockquote>The provisional government, with Khammao Vilay as prime minister and princes <b>Souvanna Phouma</b> and Souphanouvong as ministers, had little money or army and relied on support from Vietnamese communities and the Viet Minh.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-issara">Lao Issara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in Vientiane, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1954" aria-label="Luang Prabang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7306 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Souvanna Phouma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7756 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1954">What was here in 1954? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
